---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ipsas/ipsas5.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Objective, Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Benchmark vs Allowed Alternative Treatment" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Borrowing Costs Eligible for Capitalization" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Commencement, Suspension & Cessation" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Worked Examples" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Effective Date & Amendment Timeline" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Relationship to IAS 23" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Statement / Process Mapping" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="IPSAS 5 Borrowing Costs is the current, active standard issued by the IPSASB. It is effective for annual financial statements covering periods beginning on/after 1 July 2001 (earlier application encouraged). The current Standard comprises paras. 1–43, all of equal authority (paras. 2–3 deleted)."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 – OBJECTIVE, SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective, Scope &amp; Definitions (paras. 1–13)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Objective &amp; scope</div>
        <div class="principle">Prescribes the accounting treatment for borrowing costs. GENERALLY requires immediate expensing, but PERMITS, as an allowed alternative, capitalizing borrowing costs directly attributable to acquiring, constructing, or producing a qualifying asset.</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Applied in accounting for borrowing costs generally</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>Does NOT deal with the actual or imputed cost of net assets/equity – e.g. jurisdictional capital charges require judgment on whether they meet the borrowing-costs definition or are an imputed equity cost (para. 4)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Paras. 2–3 [Deleted]. All paragraphs, objective + 1–43, have equal authority.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions (para. 5)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Borrowing costs</div><div class="glossary-def">Interest and other expenses incurred by an entity in connection with the borrowing of funds.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Qualifying asset</div><div class="glossary-def">An asset that necessarily takes a SUBSTANTIAL period of time to get ready for its intended use or sale.</div></div>
        <div class="elabel">BORROWING COSTS MAY INCLUDE (PARA. 6)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Interest expense per the effective interest method (IPSAS 41)</span>
          <span>Interest on lease liabilities and service concession arrangements</span>
          <span>FX differences on foreign currency borrowings, to the extent regarded as an interest-cost adjustment</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What is (and isn't) a qualifying asset (para. 13)</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">EXAMPLES OF QUALIFYING ASSETS</div>
            <div class="dot-list"><span>Office buildings, hospitals</span><span>Infrastructure – roads, bridges, power generation facilities</span><span>Inventories requiring a substantial period to bring to a sellable/usable condition</span></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">NOT QUALIFYING ASSETS</div>
            <div class="cross-list"><span>Other investments</span><span>Assets routinely produced over a SHORT period</span><span>Assets ready for intended use/sale WHEN ACQUIRED</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Benchmark vs Allowed Alternative Treatment (paras. 14–20)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Benchmark treatment (paras. 14–16)</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Borrowing Costs → expensed in the period incurred, REGARDLESS of how the borrowings are applied</div>
        </div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The financial statements disclose the accounting policy adopted for borrowing costs (para. 16) – this disclosure applies under EITHER treatment.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Allowed alternative treatment (paras. 17–20)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Directly attributable borrowing costs are CAPITALIZED as part of the qualifying asset's cost</span>
          <span>Capitalization occurs when (a) future economic benefits/service potential are PROBABLE, and (b) costs can be measured RELIABLY</span>
          <span>Other borrowing costs are still expensed as incurred</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Once adopted, the allowed alternative treatment must be applied CONSISTENTLY to all borrowing costs directly attributable to acquiring, constructing, or producing ALL qualifying assets of the entity (para. 20) – not selectively per project.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Borrowing Costs Eligible for Capitalization (paras. 21–30)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Specific borrowings (para. 23)</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Eligible cost = Actual borrowing costs incurred − Investment income on temporary investment of those funds</div>
        </div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where funds are borrowed SPECIFICALLY for a qualifying asset, directly attributable costs are readily identifiable – "avoided cost" if the outlay hadn't been made.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">General borrowings (para. 25)</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Eligible cost = Capitalization rate × Outlays on the qualifying asset (capped at costs actually incurred in the period)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Capitalization rate = WEIGHTED AVERAGE of borrowing costs on all outstanding entity borrowings during the period – excluding specific-purpose borrowings until substantially all activities to ready that asset are complete.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Centralized / intra-economic-entity borrowing (paras. 22, 26–29)</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Difficulty arises when financing is coordinated centrally, or funds are transferred at varying/no cost across the economic entity. Key rules:</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>A controlled entity may capitalize only the borrowing costs IT ITSELF has incurred</span>
          <span>Interest-free capital contributions/transfers → no borrowing costs incurred → NOTHING to capitalize at that entity's level</span>
          <span>Partial-cost transfers → the controlled entity capitalizes its own portion; the ECONOMIC ENTITY can capitalize the FULL amount in consolidated statements, with elimination adjustments for costs already capitalized at the controlled-entity level</span>
          <span>No-cost transfers at BOTH levels → neither capitalizes; but the economic entity as a whole could still capitalize if it independently meets the criteria</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Depending on circumstances, it may be appropriate to use ALL economic-entity borrowings in the weighted average, or each controlled entity's OWN borrowings only (para. 29). Where carrying amount/expected ultimate cost exceeds recoverable amount or NRV, the excess is written down under IPSAS 21 or IPSAS 26, as appropriate (para. 30).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Commencement, Suspension &amp; Cessation of Capitalization (paras. 31–39)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Commencement (para. 31) – ALL THREE conditions</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Outlays for the asset are being incurred</span>
          <span>Borrowing costs are being incurred</span>
          <span>Activities necessary to prepare the asset for use/sale are IN PROGRESS</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">"Activities" cover more than physical construction – technical/administrative work before construction starts (e.g. obtaining permits) counts. Simply HOLDING an asset with no active development does NOT qualify (para. 33).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Suspension &amp; cessation (paras. 34–39)</div>
        <div class="elabel">SUSPENDED</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">During EXTENDED periods when active development is interrupted – those costs are expensed. NOT suspended during substantial technical/admin work, or a temporary delay that's a NECESSARY part of the process (e.g. high water levels routinely delaying bridge construction).</div>
        <div class="elabel">CEASES</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">When substantially all activities to ready the asset are COMPLETE – even if minor/routine admin work continues. For assets completed in usable PARTS (e.g. a multi-building office park), capitalization ceases PART BY PART as each becomes usable; for assets needing full completion before any use (e.g. a hospital operating theatre, a bridge), capitalization continues until the whole is done.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Worked Examples (Illustrative Examples IE1–IE16)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Example 1 – specific borrowing, qualifying asset over time</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Municipality XYZ builds a tunnel; CU100m bond at 5% funds it specifically; 3% earned on temporary investment</div>
          <div class="wk-body">CU20m paid to the contractor at construction start and each anniversary. By Dec 31, 20X1 (9 months in), accrued borrowing cost = CU100m × 5% × 9/12 = CU3.75m.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            Capitalized work-in-progress = CU20m + CU3.75m − (CU80m unused × 3% × 9/12) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">CU21.95 million</strong> – the cash transferred, plus borrowing cost incurred, less investment income earned on the temporarily-idle CU80m.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Example 3 – general borrowing, weighted average rate</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">State Government T funds an airport from a general pool: bonds CU1,000m @5%, Loan A CU300m @7%, Loan B CU600m @9%</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Total pool = CU1,900m. Weighted contribution: Bonds 1,000/1,900 × 5% = 2.63%; Loan A 300/1,900 × 7% = 1.11%; Loan B 600/1,900 × 9% = 2.84%.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            Weighted average capitalization rate = 2.63 + 1.11 + 2.84 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">6.58%</strong>, applied to outlays on the airport during the period (capped at costs actually incurred that period).
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Example 2 – centralized borrowing, market-rate loan</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Dept of Infrastructure borrows from Dept of Finance at 3% p.a. – the market rate</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Dept of Infrastructure has no visibility into how Dept of Finance itself sources funds or its weighted average cost.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Since the loan is at ARM'S-LENGTH market terms with no concessionary element, Dept of Infrastructure capitalizes borrowing costs using the contracted <strong style="color:var(--color-text-success);">3% rate</strong> – it does not need to trace back to Dept of Finance's own funding mix.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Example 4 – specific borrowing exceeding outlays</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">State Government C: CU500m federal grant + CU250m loan @5% for a road network; 3% earned on excess funds</div>
          <div class="wk-body">By Dec 31, 20X1, outlays = CU200m (paid in one lump sum at construction start), but CU250m was borrowed for the project – leaving CU50m of excess borrowed funds temporarily invested.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Capitalized cost = CU200m outlays + [(CU250m × 5%) − (CU50m × 3%)] = CU200m + <strong style="color:var(--color-text-danger);">CU11 million</strong> in net borrowing costs (CU12.5m incurred less CU1.5m investment income).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (para. 40)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Required disclosures – regardless of treatment chosen</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The accounting policy adopted for borrowing costs (benchmark or allowed alternative)</span>
          <span>The amount of borrowing costs CAPITALIZED during the period</span>
          <span>The capitalization rate used to determine eligible borrowing costs, WHEN a rate had to be applied to general borrowings</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Effective Date &amp; Amendment Timeline (paras. 41–43)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Amendment history at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">Base Standard: periods beginning on/after <strong>1 July 2001</strong> (earlier application encouraged). Para. 41 [Deleted].</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Effective</span><span>Change</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2014</span><span>Para. 6 – IPSAS 32, Service Concession Arrangements: Grantor (Oct 2011)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2017</span><span>Paras. 41, 43 – IPSAS 33, First-time Adoption</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2018</span><span>Paras. 2, 3, 11 deleted – The Applicability of IPSAS (Apr 2016)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2019</span><span>Para. 25 amended, new para. 41A – Improvements to IPSAS, 2018</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2023 (deferred from 2022 for COVID-19)</span><span>Para. 6 – Improvements to IPSAS, 2019</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2025</span><span>Para. 6 – IPSAS 43, Leases (Jan 2022)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2026</span><span>Para. 26 – IPSAS 47, Revenue (May 2023)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The IPSASB's 2019 Measurement Consultation Paper proposed REMOVING the capitalization option entirely (for centralized-borrowing comparability and GFSM alignment) – the IPSASB ultimately decided to RETAIN both options, citing consistency with the Conceptual Framework's measurement principles (Basis for Conclusions).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS – Comparison with IAS 23</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IPSAS 5 vs IAS 23</div>
        <div class="principle">IPSAS 5 is drawn primarily from IAS 23 (1993), <em>Borrowing Costs</em>.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Topic</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IPSAS 5</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">IAS 23</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Terminology</span><span>"Revenue," "statement of financial performance," "net assets/equity"</span><span>"Income," "income statement," "equity"</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Definitions (para. 5)</span><span>Distinct set of technical-term definitions</span><span>Different definition set</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">IAS 23 (as revised in 2007) eliminated the expensing option and REQUIRES capitalization. IPSAS 5 has diverged from the current IAS 23 by retaining BOTH the benchmark (expense) and allowed alternative (capitalize) treatments as a policy choice – a deliberate public-sector-specific decision reaffirmed as recently as the IPSASB's 2019 Measurement project.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Capitalization decision map (allowed alternative treatment)</div>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
          <li>Is the asset a QUALIFYING ASSET – takes a substantial period to ready for use/sale? If no &rarr; always expense.</li>
          <li>Are outlays being incurred, borrowing costs being incurred, AND preparation activities in progress? &rarr; capitalization COMMENCES</li>
          <li>Is the borrowing SPECIFIC to this asset? &rarr; eligible cost = actual cost incurred less temporary-investment income. Otherwise &rarr; apply the WEIGHTED AVERAGE capitalization rate to outlays</li>
          <li>Is active development interrupted for an EXTENDED period (not a necessary technical delay)? &rarr; SUSPEND capitalization, expense costs during the interruption</li>
          <li>Are substantially all activities to ready the asset (or a usable PART of it) complete? &rarr; capitalization CEASES for that asset/part</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>IPSAS 5 concept</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Benchmark treatment – expensed borrowing costs</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Finance costs line, per period incurred</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Allowed alternative – capitalized borrowing costs</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Added to the qualifying asset's carrying amount</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Investment income on temporary investment of borrowed funds</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Deducted from capitalized cost, not shown as separate revenue</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Write-down to recoverable amount / NRV</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Under IPSAS 21 or IPSAS 26, as appropriate</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Accounting policy, capitalized amount, capitalization rate</span><span><span class="badge be">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosures under either treatment</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Related Standards – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IPSAS 5 connects across the IPSAS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 2</span><span>Total interest paid is disclosed in the cash flow statement whether expensed or capitalized (&sect;6)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 12</span><span>Inventories requiring a substantial period to ready for sale can be qualifying assets (&sect;1)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 13 / 17</span><span>Leased and other PP&amp;E-adjacent assets referenced in the IPSAS 32 amendment history (&sect;7)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 21 / 26</span><span>Impairment write-down when carrying amount exceeds recoverable amount/NRV (&sect;3)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 32</span><span>Service Concession Arrangements: Grantor – amended the borrowing-costs component definition (&sect;7)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 41</span><span>Effective interest method for calculating interest expense (&sect;1 definitions)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 43</span><span>Interest on lease liabilities can be a borrowing cost component (&sect;1)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 45</span><span>Qualifying assets are typically items of property, plant, and equipment under construction</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Borrowing costs</div><div class="glossary-def">Interest and other expenses incurred in connection with borrowing funds.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Qualifying asset</div><div class="glossary-def">An asset needing a substantial period of time to get ready for its intended use or sale.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Capitalization rate</div><div class="glossary-def">The weighted average of borrowing costs applicable to all of the entity's outstanding general borrowings during the period.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Benchmark treatment</div><div class="glossary-def">Expensing all borrowing costs as incurred, regardless of how the borrowed funds are applied.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Allowed alternative treatment</div><div class="glossary-def">Capitalizing directly attributable borrowing costs as part of a qualifying asset's cost.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Economic entity</div><div class="glossary-def">A controlling entity and its controlled entities, for financial reporting purposes.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> Statement of financial position
      <span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span> Statement of financial performance
    </div>
  </div>

</StandardPageLayout>
